Nineteen hexagons through every edge
==================================

Read nineteen.tex/pdf for the complete bounded-order proof. The claim is
n(4,6,19) >= 36, recovering one fixed source lower-bound clause only.
The lambda20 clause is acquired separately; existence at36 is not claimed.

Replay: normal Python check.py --study STUDY --out FRESH --render.
The default freshly generates every normalized root, checks every literal
reuse and exhausts all bounded outside extensions, then runs independent
finite controls. A complete ordinary replay took about50 seconds here;
this timing is documentary, not a comparative performance claim.
Optional --sanitize repeats with address/undefined sanitizers.
C++17 and a 64-bit-word nauty library are required; nauty only proposes
maps whose full adjacency action is checked. No catalogue completeness
is inferred from a canonical key or expected count.

The acquired forest method and earlier geometry remain explicitly attributed.
Distinct review is required; no whole-original, conjecture, Lean or
measurement credit is supplied by this author packet.
